<item>
  <title>Merge Sort: A Divide-and-Conquer Algorithm</title>
  <link>https://jerryleung16.github.io/my-math-notes/posts/merge sort/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="algorithm-design-approaches" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="algorithm-design-approaches">1. Algorithm Design Approaches</h2>
<p>Algorithms can be designed using various fundamental approaches. Two primary techniques are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Incremental Approach:</strong> Used by algorithms like Insertion Sort. It builds the solution one element at a time (e.g., inserting a single element into an already sorted subarray).</li>
<li><strong>Divide-and-Conquer Approach:</strong> A highly recursive alternative that often yields significantly faster worst-case running times. It solves problems by breaking them down into smaller, manageable pieces.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="the-divide-and-conquer-paradigm" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-divide-and-conquer-paradigm">2. The Divide-and-Conquer Paradigm</h2>
<p>This recursive approach involves three distinct steps at each level of the recursion:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>Divide:</strong> Break the original problem into a number of smaller subproblems that are just smaller instances of the exact same problem.</li>
<li><strong>Conquer:</strong> Solve the subproblems recursively. However, if the subproblem size is small enough (the recursion “bottoms out”), solve it in a straightforward, direct manner.</li>
<li><strong>Combine:</strong> Merge the solutions of the smaller subproblems together to formulate the solution to the original, overarching problem.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="merge-sort-overview" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="merge-sort-overview">3. Merge Sort Overview</h2>
<p>Merge Sort is a classic algorithm that strictly follows the divide-and-conquer paradigm to sort a sequence of elements:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Divide:</strong> Split the sequence into two separate subsequences of equal (or near-equal) size.</li>
<li><strong>Conquer:</strong> Recursively sort both subsequences using Merge Sort. This process bottoms out when a sequence hits a length of 1, as a single element is already inherently sorted.</li>
<li><strong>Combine:</strong> Merge the two sorted subsequences back together to produce the final, fully sorted answer.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The Brief Idea:</strong> The algorithm works by recursively tracing the array indices all the way down to the base cases (single elements) first, and only then performs the merging process on the way back up the call stack.</p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="the-merge-sort-pseudocode" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-merge-sort-pseudocode">4. The <code>MERGE-SORT</code> Pseudocode</h2>
<p>To sort an array, we need a function that drives the divide-and-conquer process. You can refer to the provided file <strong>image.png</strong> for the standard textbook representation of this algorithm.</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<thead>
