<p>To sort an entire sequence <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%20=%20%5Clangle%20A%5B1%5D,%20A%5B2%5D,%20%5Cdots,%20A%5Bn%5D%20%5Crangle">, we make the initial call <code>MERGE-SORT(A, 1, A.length)</code>. The algorithm consists of repeatedly merging pairs of smaller sorted sequences to form larger ones until the final sorted sequence is formulated.</p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="concrete-example-tracing-merge-sort" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="concrete-example-tracing-merge-sort">5. Concrete Example: Tracing <code>MERGE-SORT</code></h2>
<p>Let us trace the overarching <code>MERGE-SORT</code> logic on a 4-element array to see how it traces indices before merging.</p>
<p><strong>Initial Array:</strong> <code>[5, 2, 4, 1]</code></p>
<p>```text MERGE-SORT(A, 1, 4) if 1 &lt; 4 (ok) q = floor((1 + 4)/2) = 2</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 1, 2) if 1 &lt; 2 (ok) q = floor((1 + 2)/2) = 1</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 1, 1) if 1 &lt; 1 (no) end</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 2, 2) if 2 &lt; 2 (no) end</p>
<p>MERGE(A, 1, 1, 2) # Array A becomes [2, 5, 4, 1]</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 3, 4) if 3 &lt; 4 (ok) q = floor((3 + 4)/2) = 3</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 3, 3) if 3 &lt; 3 (no) end</p>
<p>MERGE-SORT(A, 4, 4) if 4 &lt; 4 (no) end</p>
<p>MERGE(A, 3, 3, 4) # Array A becomes [2, 5, 1, 4]</p>
<p>MERGE(A, 1, 2, 4) # Array A becomes [1, 2, 4, 5]</p>
<p>## 6. The <code>MERGE</code> Procedure</p>
<p>The crucial operation in Merge Sort is the “combine” step, handled by the auxiliary procedure <code>MERGE(A, p, q, r)</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> It assumes that the two subarrays <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bp%20%5Cdots%20q%5D"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bq+1%20%5Cdots%20r%5D"> are already sorted. It merges them into a single sorted subarray that replaces <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bp%20%5Cdots%20r%5D">.</li>
<li><strong>Card Analogy:</strong> Imagine two sorted piles of cards face up on a table. You compare the top two cards, remove the smaller one, and place it face down in a new output pile. You repeat this constant-time comparison step until all cards are merged.</li>
<li><strong>Sentinels:</strong> To prevent the algorithm from constantly needing to check if either pile is completely empty, a special “sentinel” card containing the value infinity (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cinfty">) is placed at the bottom of each pile. This guarantees that the core sorting logic stops exactly when all real data has been merged.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
</section>
